## Pipeline

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Admin uploads<br/>PDF / TXT / MD] --> B[Chunk + embed<br/>1024-dim]
  B --> C[(Pinecone)]
  D[User question] --> E[Top-5 retrieval]
  C --> E
  E --> F[Grounded prompt<br/>with citations]
  F --> G[Streaming answer<br/>via Vercel AI SDK]
```

## Why this exists

Support chatbots that wrap a model directly invent answers the moment a question goes off-topic. For a support inbox that's worse than no bot at all. This one defaults to refusal: answer only from indexed documents, cite the source file, and say "I don't know" when retrieval finds nothing relevant.

## What's built

- **Drag-and-drop ingestion.** Admin uploads a file; the server parses, chunks, embeds, and writes vectors to Pinecone with the source filename in metadata. Postgres tracks chunk counts so the admin UI shows "doc X — 42 chunks" and supports clean delete.
- **Retrieval-augmented prompting.** Each user message triggers a top-5 vector lookup; matching chunks are stitched into the system prompt as a `## Knowledge base` block.
- **Streaming chat UI** via the Vercel AI SDK.
- **Persona with rules.** The system prompt defines a persona but spends most of its words on what *not* to do — no guessing, no competitor talk, escalate after three failed attempts.
- **Admin console at `/admin`** with chunk counts, delete, and upload-type validation.

## Technical choices

### "Don't know" is the default

If retrieval returns zero relevant chunks, the prompt builder strips the knowledge-base block and the persona's refusal rule takes over. This pushes the model toward refusing rather than confabulating — the most common production failure mode for support bots.

### Source attribution baked into the prompt

Every retrieved chunk is prefixed with `[1] (from: returns-policy.pdf)`. The prompt instructs the model to mention file names naturally ("According to our returns policy…") so users see the source without needing a UI for it.

### Two-store design

Pinecone holds the vectors and chunk text; Neon Postgres holds the canonical document record. Listing in the admin doesn't hit Pinecone, and delete cascades both stores in one server action.

## Outcome

The same RAG pipeline drops into client products that need grounded Q&A — internal knowledge bases, customer support, document portals — without rewriting the persona logic or the two-store model.

